# Shell State Is Shared

The shell session used by tool calls is **shared with the user's other terminals**. The current working directory and current branch can change between tool calls without warning — the user may switch branches in another terminal while you're working, or you may have moved earlier in the session.

## Always re-verify before branch-sensitive operations

Before `git commit`, `git push`, `gh pr create`, `gh pr merge`, or any other operation that depends on the current branch or working directory:

```bash
# Cheap sanity check — run it inline at the top of the command
[ "$(git branch --show-current)" = "<expected-branch>" ] || { echo "WRONG BRANCH — aborting"; exit 1; }
```

## Real failures this rule prevents

- **May 14 — commit on `develop-retail`**: I cherry-picked a Patrick-feedback commit while the shell had drifted from `LSR-37242-Data-layer-cust-e-invoicing` to `develop-retail` because the user had been on a different feature branch in another terminal. The commit landed on `develop-retail` instead of the feature branch.

- **May 19 — stale-working-tree docs PR**: My local `/Volumes/dev/r-series-core-docs/main` checkout had drifted from `origin/main` in the file tree (not the HEAD). `git pull --rebase` reported "Already up to date" but the working file was a week old. A `StrReplace` was added on top, and the commit captured 9 unintended PR-#83 reversions along with the intended one-line change.

## When you spot drift

- Print branch + status before doing anything destructive.
- If the working tree has unexpected modifications you didn't make, `git reset --hard origin/<your-branch>` and re-apply intentionally.
- Don't trust `git pull --rebase` "Already up to date" as proof of cleanliness — it doesn't check uncommitted working-tree drift.
